<div class="pagehead">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h1>Accessibility</h1>
    <p>What this site does to stay usable with a keyboard, a screen reader or a magnifier, and where it still falls short.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h2>Target</h2>
    <p>
      This site is built against WCAG 2.2 level AA. That is the target, not a certification. It has not been
      audited by an external accessibility firm, and that is stated plainly rather than implied away.
    </p>

    <h2>What is in place</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Every page works without JavaScript. Navigation, search, the basket and both forms are ordinary links and form posts.</li>
      <li>A skip link is the first focusable element on every page.</li>
      <li>Every form control has a visible label that is programmatically associated with it. Placeholders are never used as labels.</li>
      <li>Errors are listed in a summary at the top of the form, each error links to the field, and the field itself is marked with <code>aria-invalid</code>.</li>
      <li>Focus is always visible, with a three pixel outline that is not removed anywhere in the stylesheet.</li>
      <li>Buttons say what they do. There is no button on this site labelled only <q>Submit</q>, <q>Click here</q> or <q>Go</q>.</li>
      <li>Product images carry descriptive alternative text. Decorative images carry an empty alt attribute so screen readers skip them.</li>
      <li>Text contrast meets or exceeds 4.5 to 1 against its background.</li>
      <li>No animation runs on scroll, no custom cursor is drawn, and transitions are disabled entirely for anyone whose system asks for reduced motion.</li>
      <li>The page still works at 400 percent zoom and reflows to a single column without a horizontal scrollbar.</li>
      <li>Data tables use real table markup with scoped headers and captions.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Known gaps</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Some product photographs come from the supplier with text baked into the image. That text is not available to a screen reader.</li>
      <li>Alternative text for product images is generated from the product name and specification rather than written by a person describing the photograph.</li>
      <li>No formal audit or user testing with assistive technology has been carried out yet.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Telling us about a problem</h2>
    <p>
      If something on this site blocks you, describe what you were trying to do and what got in the way
      through the <a href="/contact">contact form</a>. Accessibility faults are treated as bugs, not as
      feature requests.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

